/-
Johnson/G/DryProofLit.lean: the three lists of rounds that the dry runs of a proof file's three
blocks state, at the full-size and at the small example, as written values, for statements that
Lean's kernel is to evaluate.

The makers of a proof file's protocol run their tests on these lists. The lists are computed from
public files by parsing, which Lean's kernel does not evaluate. Here they stand written out: the
distinct rounds once, and each list as runs. Definitions, and theorems on lengths by an evaluation
in Lean's kernel.
Main names:
  `dryProofFull`, `dryProofSmall : Rounds × Rounds × Rounds`: the three lists.
    `dryProofFull_length`, `dryProofSmall_length`: their lengths.
Whence the numbers: a program wrote the lists from a print that Lean's evaluator made of
`OnBytes.Maker.dryProof` at the proof's chains and at the first chain's block
(`OnBytes.Proof.firstBlock` over `OnBytes.Model.Stages.runLinksH`) of a made-up request: T tokens 0,
the covered positions 0 to T - 1, the reply 0 (not the request `firstInstance`,
Johnson/Flat/LinksAt.lean, which covers the last position alone), under the parameters that
`OnBytes.Model.Verify.planned` gives at the three shipped public files policy.txt, layout.txt and
KL.bin of prover/examples/target_full_f2, and at the library's own copies of those of
prover/examples/target_small_f2_roomy; at the key of zeros of the table's shape. No other file was
read.
Limits: the theorems speak of the written lists. This file by itself holds them against nothing. At
the full size no test that a build runs holds them against the program's dry runs; at
the small example none is named here. The side library holds the data made of them
against the proof file's data in the verifier's description at
`OnBytes.secondHashFunction` and `VerifierAndGoals.storedPublicFiles`: `written_zFull`
(AtFullSide/Top/WrittenAtFull.lean, by Lean's kernel alone). The dry runs were made at
one key of the table and one request. The rounds hang on one number of the key: the
statement's item under which the table's root stands, which the round "LOC" writes into
the stored table that it opens: it is 0 here because the key of zeros says 0. That the
dry runs state the same rounds at every key of that item that fits is shown in
OnBytes/Maker/FilesFree.lean (`keyAlike_of_fits`, `dryProof_keyAlike`), not here; that
they do so at every request is not shown here. -/
import Johnson.G.DryKeyLit
import Johnson.G.Eager

